N2  - Summary The ubiquity offered by the integration of MTC and satellite networks is key to the pervasiveness of 6G. However, the infrastructure technology landscape presents a wide variation in terms of connectivity options. On the one hand, MTC is largely anchored by a variety of LPWAN technologies such as LoRa, Sigfox, and 3GPP-based access, while, on the other hand, LEO and GEO satellites and mega-constellations all crowd the satellite domain. Therefore, there is a critical need for a unifying fabric that delivers network orchestration, management, and control in an end-to-end manner across heterogeneous infrastructure. Widely expected to play a major enabling role within 6G, SDN is a prime candidate for the realization of this unifying fabric. To that end, this chapter of the book explores the role of SDN in MTC and satellite connectivity by presenting SpaceFabric???a novel SDN -centered fabric for the orchestration, management, and control of satellite-integrated MTC networks. The key highlights include a robust treatment of the state of the art, a comprehensive overview of the required key enabling technologies, and a detailed architecture description of SpaceFabric along with implementation considerations. Furthermore, the chapter also includes an extensive discussion regarding MTC -specific use cases that would benefit from SpaceFabric, along with a set of research challenges that are critical to the realization of the proposed system.
